---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { href } from "../data/site";
---
<Base title="Governance" description="How the orchestra handles data, licences, record-keeping and refusals: the four classes of data, keeping GRDC code and data out of the packages, per-package licences, the independent-check rule and the recorded refusal to answer." path="/governance">
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="kicker">Governance</p>
      <h1>Data, licences, record-keeping and refusals</h1>
      <p class="prose lead mt-2">Four rules make the orchestra's results safe to act on and safe to share.
        They are set out here so that a research manager, a data custodian or a reviewer can check them
        without reading code.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2 class="mt-3">Classes of data</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Public.</strong> Published trial datasets, public climate and soil services, and crop
          simulators. This is the only class of data used in package examples, tests and vignettes (the worked
          tutorials that come with each package).</li>
        <li><strong>Synthetic, with a known right answer.</strong> Generated sets of sites and multi-environment
          trials built around a right answer chosen in advance, so a method can be marked on whether it recovers
          it. Every study in the series runs on one.</li>
        <li><strong>Synthetic copy made by the data owner.</strong> The owner uses masque to make a synthetic
          copy of a real dataset that keeps the real data's structure. The analysis is developed on the copy and
          then rerun on the real rows by the custodian; only summary figures leave. No real row enters a package,
          a page or a poster.</li>
        <li><strong>Restricted.</strong> Data restricted by ethics approval or by contract. It never enters the
          orchestra's packages, tests, sites or publications, in any form.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="mt-3">No GRDC code or data in the packages</h2>
      <p class="mt-1">No code or data that came from the GRDC (Grains Research and Development Corporation)
        enters any orchestra package, study output or public page. Where a GRDC-approved dataset is used for
        research, it is used only through a synthetic copy made by the owner, only summary figures leave, and
        publishing those figures needs the GRDC manager's agreement. The packages are deliberately general, and
        that is what lets a GRDC analysis pipeline use them.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-3">Licences and where the packages are published</h2>
      <p class="mt-1">The packages are open source, under MIT or GPL depending on the package, and are published,
        or being prepared for publishing, under the <code>max578</code> account, with an R-universe channel (a
        repository from which R users can install them). An AAGI-AUS copy of this site and of the packages is
        prepared but not yet released: no licence is chosen for that copy until the licence has been discussed
        with the GRDC manager and all interested parties.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-3">Where every result came from</h2>
      <p class="mt-1">Every result is saved as a manifest, the shared result record every package writes. It
        holds the package that produced it and its version, the quantity being estimated, the sampled parameter
        values that describe how uncertain the result is, the outputs, a checksum (a fingerprint of the contents
        that changes if anything is altered), and a list of the earlier records it was built from. A record that
        has been altered fails the check, a record built from others without that list is rejected, and a report
        built from these records can link every printed number to the record it came from.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-3">The independent-check rule</h2>
      <p class="mt-1">Every fact about an outside source (a service's response format, a dataset's columns, a
        constant, a citation) is checked against a reference the orchestra did not produce itself. A test whose
        expected answer comes from the same place as the claim shows only that the claim agrees with itself, not
        that it is true. Facts that have not been checked are marked as not confirmed, and the decision step
        refuses to put a price on them. This is how a crop-model estimate that field results have contradicted
        never becomes a nitrogen rate.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-3">Recording a refusal to answer</h2>
      <p class="mt-1">When a package declines to answer, it returns a record saying so and why, not an error
        message. Every package can produce one, every package that receives results can recognise one with a
        single check, and a test run across all packages triggers one real refusal in each. Making a decision
        on top of a refusal breaks the rules every package follows, and the decision step does not do it.</p>

      <p class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/a0")}>Study A0 results</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/about")}>About</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
